From: =?UTF-8?q?=EC=8B=AC=EC=83=81=EC=9B=90?= <dev.chamomile@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 23:07:16 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] macos: cpu-template-helper's fingerprint names what a guest
 of the host reads

The fingerprint reads three Linux sysfs files about the host. macOS has
none of them, so `fingerprint dump` failed at the first one ("Failed to
read .../revidr_el1: No such file or directory"). On macOS
read_sysfs_file now gives each file from macOS's sources, holding what
the field tells of a guest's host. Linux's code path is unchanged; the
read_to_string import and read_sysfs_file are left out on macOS as on
Windows.

- revidr_el1 (the field microcode_version on aarch64):
  - On Linux this is the host's REVIDR_EL1, and a KVM guest reads the
    same value (v6.1 arch/arm64/kvm/sys_regs.c, invariant_sys_regs).
  - The host's REVIDR_EL1 cannot be read on macOS. It is an EL1 register,
    no sysctl gives it, and Hypervisor.framework has no register for it
    (hv_vcpu_types.h).
  - So the file holds what every guest reads under Hypervisor.framework:
    0, in Linux's "0x%016llx" (arch/arm64/kernel/cpuinfo.c).
  - wick's boot-test `fingerprint` stage reads it in a guest and compares.
- bios_version: Apple silicon has no SMBIOS. The file holds
  IODeviceTree:/chosen's system-firmware-version, the Mac's firmware
  ("mBoot-20457.41.3"), read through IOKit. A missing entry or property is
  an error naming it.
- bios_release: SMBIOS's BIOS release is the firmware's major.minor
  release ("%u.%u", drivers/firmware/dmi_scan.c). The file holds that of
  the firmware version ("20457.41"). A version without one is refused.

Upstream test it unlocks: test_consecutive_fingerprint_consistency
(passes, macOS 27.2). fingerprint::dump::tests::test_read_valid_sysfs_file
passes too. New test: test_macos_firmware_is_ioregs (the values ioreg shows,
and the refusals).

Still different: a change of the host CPU's revision does not show in
microcode_version, which is the same on every Mac. The CPU configuration's
MIDR_EL1 is Hypervisor.framework's generic Apple MIDR (0x610f0000), what
the guest reads, not the host's part number.
